Observation models, inverse questions, and Bayesian interpretation (@met-inverse-bayesian: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @metrology, #source "../sources/Uncertainty-NIST-1900.lingua#r_75WCVW8CJG4TAFD3330T87588E", #source "../sources/GUM-Models.lingua#r_3AAJPGTHAAF72R05SJ1VJ7HN46", #source "../sources/GUM-Monte-Carlo.lingua#r_79V20QP3T2KZ6GD07SK5TGZ1J4", #metrology "Sampling-and-censoring.lingua#r_3RRJRMNNAPMVYJ9Z6VGY2DV0CG", #industrial-gases-and-vacuum "../../../utilities/industrial-gases-vacuum-and-high-purity-supplies/measurement/When-a-spectrum-cannot-identify-a-mixture.lingua#r_5AQF13GWSK9SYF1V68GPWS2M1T", #clean-manufacturing "../../../production/clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control/aerosol-measurement/Inversion-cannot-create-information-that-the-response-loses.lingua#r_7EQ5WTVSRCQPA7WYPQ0YVTHTYX") { r_1TSJYTGBD1NY9Q7VP0BDFXZ94K
**Read:** [Simple Guide for Evaluating and Expressing Uncertainty — Possolo](../sources/Uncertainty-NIST-1900.lingua#r_75WCVW8CJG4TAFD3330T87588E), sections 3–8 and contrasting observation-equation examples; [Developing and using measurement models — JCGM GUM-6](../sources/GUM-Models.lingua#r_3AAJPGTHAAF72R05SJ1VJ7HN46), clause 11, including Bayesian statistical models; [Propagation of distributions using Monte Carlo — JCGM 101](../sources/GUM-Monte-Carlo.lingua#r_79V20QP3T2KZ6GD07SK5TGZ1J4), the distribution-assignment discussion.

Some measurement questions estimate a parameter or latent quantity from a probability model for observations rather than from a simple explicit equation with already characterized inputs. The likelihood describes observations conditional on the unknowns and conditions. A Bayesian analysis combines it with prior information to produce a posterior distribution. Different inferential approaches must state what their intervals and probabilities mean.

For independent normal observations with known variance σ², sample size n, sample mean x̄, and a normal prior for θ with mean μ₀ and variance τ², posterior variance is v = (1/τ² + n/σ²)^−1 and posterior mean is v(μ₀/τ² + nx̄/σ²). These expressions are a solvable teaching benchmark, not a general rule for every inverse problem.

**Work:** derive the benchmark by completing the square in the product of likelihood and prior. With μ₀ = 0, τ² = 4, σ² = 1, n = 4, and x̄ = 1 in consistent units, obtain posterior mean 16/17 and variance 4/17. Compare a much wider prior and a much narrower one. Explain what real evidence could support the prior and how sensitivity to it would be reported.

Now consider an unknown sensor input inferred through uncertain calibration parameters. Include those parameters and their dependence instead of fixing them at best-fit values. Examine identifiability, model mismatch, and prior influence where the data are weak. A numerically stable posterior does not prove that the likelihood represents the experiment.

Compare one observation-equation example in TN 1900 with an explicit measurement-equation example. State the inferential choices and reconstruct the published calculation. General probability, statistics, optimization, and inverse-problem theory deepen in their shared mathematical subjects; the metrology requirement is to understand and defend the particular inference used for the result.

